//? Title: Weighted covariance and standardized sample moments
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

Weights can express the relative influence of a row, or the number of
times that row appears in a sample. Both interpretations give the same
weighted mean. The sample covariance uses a different divisor in each case.
Frequency weights reproduce a sample with explicitly repeated rows.

```{cpp, id=sample-moments, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/sample_moments.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const datamunge::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> x(2, 2, {1, 2, 3, 6});
    const auto analytic = weighted_sample_moments(x, {2, 1});
    const auto frequency = weighted_sample_moments(x, {2, 1}, SampleWeightType::Frequency);
    std::println("| Weight type | Mean of column 1 | Covariance of columns 1 and 2 |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| Analytic | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", analytic.mean[0], analytic.covariance(0, 1));
    std::println("| Frequency | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", frequency.mean[0], frequency.covariance(0, 1));

    const auto raw = sample_shape({1, 2, 3, 10});
    const auto corrected = sample_shape({1, 2, 3, 10}, true);
    std::println("\n| Method | Skewness | Excess kurtosis |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| Sample moment coefficients | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", raw.skewness, raw.excess_kurtosis);
    std::println("| Finite-sample corrections | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                 corrected.skewness, corrected.excess_kurtosis);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Weight type | Mean of column 1 | Covariance of columns 1 and 2 |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Analytic | 1.667 | 4.000 |
| Frequency | 1.667 | 2.667 |

| Method | Skewness | Excess kurtosis |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Sample moment coefficients | 1.018 | -0.770 |
| Finite-sample corrections | 1.764 | 3.228 |
// result_end

Skewness measures asymmetry. Excess kurtosis compares the standardized
fourth moment with the normal distribution's value of three. With only four
observations, the finite-sample correction can substantially change the estimate.

> Summarize spread and concentration

The second L-moment is half the mean absolute difference over distinct
pairs. The Lorenz curve instead accumulates weighted value from smallest to
largest, and its Gini coefficient summarizes the gap from equal shares.

```{cpp, id=spread-and-concentration, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/sample_moments.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const auto moments = sample_l_moments({2, 4, 9, 15, 63});
    std::println("| L-location | L-scale | L-skewness | L-kurtosis |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} |\n",
                 moments.location, moments.scale, *moments.skewness, *moments.kurtosis);

    const auto curve = lorenz_curve({1, 3}, {3, 1});
    std::println("The smallest {:.0f}% of the weight accounts for {:.0f}% of the value. Gini = {:.2f}.",
                 100 * curve.population_share[1], 100 * curve.value_share[1], curve.gini);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| L-location | L-scale | L-skewness | L-kurtosis |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 18.60 | 13.30 | 0.70 | 0.59 |

The smallest 75% of the weight accounts for 50% of the value. Gini = 0.25.
// result_end

> Propagate uncertainty through a transformation

The covariance of derived estimates depends on their joint derivatives.
For the sum and difference of two fitted parameters, the derivative rows
are `[1, 1]` and `[1, -1]`. Their covariance can be nonzero even when all
derivatives are fixed.

A ratio needs particular care when the denominator is uncertain. Fieller's
method tests each possible ratio and can return two unbounded intervals.

```{cpp, id=transformed-uncertainty, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/sample_moments.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const auto cov = delta_method_covariance({2, 2, {1, 1, 1, -1}}, {2, 2, {1, .5, .5, 2}});
    std::println("| | Sum | Difference |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| Sum | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |", cov(0, 0), cov(0, 1));
    std::println("| Difference | {:.0f} | {:.0f} |", cov(1, 0), cov(1, 1));

    const auto intervals = fieller_confidence_set(2, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1);
    std::println("\nWith a denominator estimate of zero and critical value 1, the ratio set is "
                 "[{:.0f}, {:.0f}] together with [{:.0f}, {:.0f}].",
                 intervals[0].first, intervals[0].second,
                 intervals[1].first, intervals[1].second);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| | Sum | Difference |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Sum | 4 | -1 |
| Difference | -1 | 2 |

With a denominator estimate of zero and critical value 1, the ratio set is [-inf, -2] together with [2, inf].
// result_end
